# Review routing for CALIPER.
#
# Every path is owned by A, so every pull request from B, C or D requires A's
# approval before it can merge. GitHub does not let anyone approve their own
# pull request, which is the point: a write collaborator cannot open a change
# and merge it unreviewed.
#
# A's own pull requests are the deliberate asymmetry. A is the only code owner,
# so they cannot be approved by a code owner at all; A merges them on the
# administrator bypass instead (branch protection has "include administrators"
# off). That keeps the engine work on the critical path from waiting on a
# review by someone who does not yet have the context for it — proposal §12
# names a foundational slip in the first phase as the principal schedule risk.
#
# The asymmetry is recorded rather than hidden: A reviews the team, the team
# does not review A. Revisit it once the corpus lands and B has context on the
# engine, or narrow ownership to spec/ and docs/ if the review load bites.

